CCCC VM: performance evaluation & FP analysis

  1. How it stacks up

  The numbers (from your own BENCHMARKS.md, geomean ~245× slower than gcc -O2) place this squarely in the simple bytecode-interpreter
  class. To set expectations for the fork, here's where that sits — these are architecture-class reference points, not
  measured-on-this-machine figures (I can't run Lua/CPython here):

  ┌───────────────────────────┬───────────────────────┬──────────────────────────────────┐
  │           Class           │ Typical slowdown vs C │             Examples             │
  ├───────────────────────────┼───────────────────────┼──────────────────────────────────┤
  │ JIT (tracing/method)      │ 1–5×                  │ LuaJIT, V8, JSC, PyPy, JVM, .NET │
  ├───────────────────────────┼───────────────────────┼──────────────────────────────────┤
  │ Lean register interpreter │ ~15–50×               │ Lua 5.4, Wren                    │
  ├───────────────────────────┼───────────────────────┼──────────────────────────────────┤
  │ Boxed-value interpreter   │ ~30–100×              │ CPython 3.11                     │
  ├───────────────────────────┼───────────────────────┼──────────────────────────────────┤
  │ CCCC today                │ ~200–450×             │ —                                │
  └───────────────────────────┴───────────────────────┴──────────────────────────────────┘

  So you're roughly 5–10× slower than a lean scripting interpreter like Lua 5.4, despite having the right bones (register ISA,
  computed-goto, fused load/store, scalar promotion). That gap is the interesting part — it means there's recoverable headroom in the
  interpreter itself, separate from the "you'd need a JIT" ceiling.

  Two structural taxes explain most of the 5–10×:

  Per-dispatch prologue. Every single instruction pays, before reaching the opcode body (vm.c:508–604): unconditional vm->cycle++, a
  pending-signal poll, a CCCC_ENABLE_DEBUGGER flag test, a PC-sentinel check, an opcode bounds check (3 comparisons), a
  vm_profile_enabled test, and a debug_vm test. That's ~5 runtime branches + a counter write per opcode. A lean interpreter does
  read-op; goto. For your fork — which won't carry the safety/debugger mandate — these should be compile-time gated (#ifdef) out of
  the hot loop, not runtime-branched. Lua/LuaJIT keep the dispatch prologue at essentially zero.

  MOV3 volume. In matrix_mul, MOV3 is 23% of all opcodes — pure register shuffling the allocator didn't elide. That's a
  regalloc-quality signal, independent of FP.

  2. The FP question — and why the table looks contradictory

  Your instinct was the tagged FReg union (cccc.h:1246, 24 bytes vs 8 for a raw double; every read branches on tag in
  cccc_freg_get_f64, internal.h:208). It is a real tax — but the benchmark table rules it out as the outlier cause, and the profiler
  shows exactly why. Note that the two "FP-heavy" benchmarks behave completely differently:

  mandelbrot (219× — dead average) is genuinely FP-bound:
  FLDR_LOCAL 26.6%  FMUL3 12.3%  FSTR_LOCAL 6.2%  FADD3 6.1%  FLDR 4.3%  FSUB3 2.1%
  → FP math + FP load/store ≈ 55–60% of all opcodes
  Here the tag tax bites — FLDR_LOCAL→FMUL3 alone is 12% of all instruction transitions, each carrying a tag-branch + 24-byte access.
  This is where detagging pays.

  matrix_mul (1130× — the outlier) is not FP-bound at all:
  MOV3 23%  LDR_LOCAL_D 13%  LDR_D 8%  ADD3 7.7%  ADDI3 5%  LDA3 5%  MUL3 5%  ...
  FADD3 2.55%  FMUL3 2.52%   ← the actual floating-point math is ~5% of opcodes
  ~95% of the dispatches are address arithmetic, indexing, and moves — ~40 opcodes per FLOP, of which 2 are the float math. The tag
  is irrelevant here. The 1130× outlier comes from two things the reference compiler does that the interpreter can't: gcc -O2/-O3
  autovectorizes + FMA-contracts + cache-blocks that triple loop, and the interpreter does one scalar element per dispatch. sieve
  (417×, and it's integer) is the same story — gcc vectorizes it.

  So the answer to "how do other VMs speed up FP-heavy code" splits by which bottleneck:

