  4. Recommendation order for the fork

  1. Strip the dispatch prologue (compile-time gate cycle counter / debugger / profile / bounds checks). Cheap, helps every
  benchmark, removes the lean-interpreter gap.
  2. Detag fregs (raw doubles + separate vector regs). Cheap, directly targets mandelbrot-class FP-load/store cost. Verify the
  convert-path caveat first.
  3. FMADD3 float opcode + use the existing SIMD tags in codegen. This is what closes the matrix_mul/sieve outliers, because it's
  what gcc is doing that you aren't.
  4. Then decide whether you're building a JIT tier — that's the only thing that takes you out of the 100×+ regime, and your
  GC/threading rewrite is the natural moment to architect for it (tiering interacts with GC safepoints and your GIL removal).

  One framing caveat to carry into the writeup: don't pin FP slowness on the tag. The profiler says the tag matters for mandelbrot
  but is ~5%-of-opcodes noise for matrix_mul; the matrix_mul gap is "loops gcc vectorizes and the VM can't." Different fix per case.

JIT, copy-and-patch, and "lean" interpreters — and why each beats us

  The throughline: our cost is per-bytecode dispatch overhead, and each of these techniques removes a different slice of it. Recall
  the matrix_mul profile — ~40 opcodes per FLOP, only 2 of them the actual float math. The other 38 are the tax. Here's how each
  approach attacks it.

  What we are (baseline): a register-based threaded-dispatch interpreter

  Per bytecode we pay: read the opcode word → bounds-check it → branch through goto *op_table[op] → decode operand fields
  (shifts/masks) → do the work → jump back. The real arithmetic is a sliver of that. Two structural taxes on top (from the eval
  earlier):

  - Dispatch cost per op — even with computed-goto, every op is an indirect branch the CPU must predict. With ~40 ops per FLOP,
  that's ~40 indirect branches where native code has ~1.
  - No cross-op knowledge — each opcode body is isolated. FMUL3 then FADD3 can't keep the product in a CPU register across the two;
  it round-trips through the fregs[] array in memory. A compiler keeps it in xmm0.

  Everything below is a way of deleting dispatches and keeping values in real registers across operations.

  1. "Lean" interpreters (Lua 5.4, Wren) — same idea as us, less overhead per op

  These are also bytecode interpreters — no native codegen. They're ~15-50× off C while we're ~200-450×. The difference is constant
  factors on the exact loop we run:

  - Tiny dispatch prologue. Our hot loop pays, every instruction: an unconditional vm->cycle++, a pending-signal poll, a
  CCCC_ENABLE_DEBUGGER test, an opcode bounds-check, a vm_profile_enabled test, a debug_vm test — ~5 branches before the work. Lua
  does essentially read-op; dispatch. (This is exactly the fork-only prologue-stripping item — they get it for free by not carrying
  our runtime-switchable instrumentation mandate.)
  - Fewer, fatter opcodes. Lua's operand decode is a single word with fixed A/B/C field positions; its arithmetic ops read straight
  from the register window. We split work across more, thinner opcodes (hence 40/FLOP).
  - 8-byte values, no tag-branch on read. Lua's TValue is a NaN-boxed or tag+union 16-byte slot, but numeric ops read the double
  directly. Our FReg is 24 bytes and branches on the tag every read — which is precisely #460.

  So a lean interpreter isn't doing anything magic; it's running our design with the fat trimmed off. #459/#460/#461 are us moving
  toward that class without abandoning the interpreter model. That's the realistic ceiling for cccc: closing toward Lua-tier
  (~15-50×), not toward JIT-tier.

  2. JIT with LLVM (JVM C2, Julia, older WebKit FTL) — maximum quality, slow to produce

  A JIT translates hot bytecode into native machine code at runtime, so the 40 dispatches collapse into a handful of real
  instructions with values living in hardware registers. The LLVM flavor specifically:

  - You build LLVM IR for the hot function, hand it to LLVM's optimizer + backend, get native code back, and patch the call site to
  jump there.
  - Why it's fast: you inherit LLVM's entire optimization pipeline — register allocation, autovectorization (the thing that makes gcc
  crush matrix_mul), FMA contraction, loop unrolling, inlining. The output rivals gcc -O2. This is how you'd get to ~1-3× of C
  instead of ~200×.
  - Why nobody puts it on the hot path raw: LLVM compilation is enormously expensive — milliseconds-to-seconds per function. You only
  amortize it on code that runs millions of times, so LLVM JITs are tiered (interpret first, LLVM-compile only the provably-hot
  bits). Big runtime dependency, big memory footprint.
  - Why not for cccc: it breaks the "single self-contained interpretable binary" mandate and the "every op is instrumentable for
  safety/debug" model — JITted native code doesn't run your CHKB/CHKP3 opcodes. This is a fork option, and even there it's the
  heavyweight choice.

  3. Copy-and-patch (CPython 3.13's JIT, Wasm baseline tiers) — 90% of the win, ~0.1% of the build cost

  This is the interesting middle, and the one I'd actually point the fork at. The insight: most of a JIT's output quality, almost
  none of its compile cost.

  - Ahead of time (at your build time, not the user's runtime), you compile each bytecode handler into a machine-code stencil — a
  pre-baked snippet of native code with holes where the operands/jump-targets go. These come out of the C compiler offline; LLVM
  never runs at the user's runtime.
  - At runtime, "compiling" a function is just: for each bytecode, memcpy the matching stencil into an executable buffer and patch
  the holes (fill in the register numbers, immediate values, the address of the next stencil). That's it — a copy and a few pointer
  writes per op. Compilation is linear and trivially cheap, microseconds not milliseconds.
  - Why it's faster than us: the stencils are contiguous native code. The per-op indirect-branch dispatch disappears — one stencil
  falls through to the next, the CPU's branch predictor and instruction prefetch sail straight through. Values can stay in pinned
  hardware registers across stencils (CPython pins the top of stack). You've removed the ~40 indirect branches per FLOP and the
  memory round-trips, without an optimizing backend at runtime.
  - The tradeoff vs LLVM: the code quality is roughly "good -O0/-O1" — no autovectorization, no cross-op instruction scheduling. So
  copy-and-patch won't autovectorize matrix_mul the way LLVM/gcc does; it mainly kills dispatch overhead. It lands you maybe ~3-10×
  of C — between lean-interpreter and full-JIT — for near-zero compile latency and no runtime LLVM dependency.
  - Why it fits a fork well: the stencils are generated from your existing opcode bodies, so it composes with the interpreter rather
  than replacing it (you keep the interpreter as tier-0, copy-and-patch as tier-1). And because a stencil is just the handler's
  machine code, your safety-check opcodes can survive as stencils too — unlike a from-scratch LLVM JIT.

  The one-line mental model

  ┌────────────────────┬───────────────────┬─────────────────────┬──────────────────────┬─────────────────┐
  │     Technique      │ Removes dispatch? │ Optimizing backend? │ Runtime compile cost │ Lands at (vs C) │
  ├────────────────────┼───────────────────┼─────────────────────┼──────────────────────┼─────────────────┤
  │ Our VM today       │ no                │ no                  │ none                 │ ~200-450×       │
  ├────────────────────┼───────────────────┼─────────────────────┼──────────────────────┼─────────────────┤
  │ Lean interpreter   │ no (just cheaper) │ no                  │ none                 │ ~15-50×         │
  ├────────────────────┼───────────────────┼─────────────────────┼──────────────────────┼─────────────────┤
  │ Copy-and-patch JIT │ yes               │ no                  │ ~microseconds        │ ~3-10×          │
  ├────────────────────┼───────────────────┼─────────────────────┼──────────────────────┼─────────────────┤
  │ LLVM JIT           │ yes               │ yes (autovec, FMA)  │ ~ms-seconds          │ ~1-3×           │
  └────────────────────┴───────────────────┴─────────────────────┴──────────────────────┴─────────────────┘

  For cccc: tickets #459-#463 walk us toward the lean-interpreter column without leaving the interpreter model — that's the right
  ceiling here. For the fork: copy-and-patch is the high-leverage next tier (keeps your interpreter as tier-0, kills the dispatch
  tax, no LLVM dependency); reach for an LLVM tier only if you specifically need autovectorized numeric throughput.

